# ## 4. Dimensionality Reduction
# 
# ### 4.1 PCA (Principal Component Analysis)
# 
# **What is PCA?**
# - Linear transformation that finds directions of maximum variance
# - Reduces 2048 dimensions to fewer components while preserving variance
# - First component = direction of highest variance, second = second highest, etc.
# 
# **Our approach:**
# - Reduce to 50 dimensions (retaining ~73% variance)
# - Then use t-SNE on these 50 dimensions for 2D visualization
# 
# **Why 50 components?**
# 
# **Analysis performed**: Tested 10, 20, 30, 50, 75, 100, 150, 200 components
# 
# **Key Finding - The Curse of Dimensionality:**
# - ✅ **ARI score is IDENTICAL (0.4041) across ALL dimensions** (10 to 200 components)
# - ⚠️ **Silhouette score DECREASES as dimensions increase**:
#   - 10 components (42.5% variance): Silhouette = 0.2032 (BEST)
#   - 50 components (73.5% variance): Silhouette = 0.1189 (our choice)
#   - 200 components (92.9% variance): Silhouette = 0.0940 (WORST)
# 
# **Why this happens:**
# - In high dimensions, Euclidean distances become less meaningful
# - K-Means performance degrades with more dimensions
# - Extra variance often contains noise, not useful signal
# - First 10-20 components capture the most discriminative features
# 
# **Why we chose 50 components:**
# - ✅ Balance between clustering quality and information retention
# - ✅ Same weak label quality as 10 components (identical ARI)
# - ✅ More features for downstream neural network training (Notebook 3)
# - ✅ Conventional choice in practice
# - ⚠️ Slightly worse Silhouette than fewer components (acceptable trade-off)
# 
# **Lesson**: More variance ≠ Better clustering. Less is often more!

# In[4]:


# Apply PCA to reduce to 50 components
print("Applying PCA...")
pca_50 = PCA(n_components=50, random_state=SEED)
features_pca_50 = pca_50.fit_transform(features_scaled)

# Calculate cumulative explained variance
cumulative_variance = np.cumsum(pca_50.explained_variance_ratio_)

print(f"\n✓ PCA completed")
print(f"  - Original dimensions: {features_scaled.shape[1]}")
print(f"  - Reduced dimensions: {features_pca_50.shape[1]}")
print(f"  - Variance explained by 50 components: {cumulative_variance[-1]:.2%}")
print(f"  - Variance explained by first 10 components: {cumulative_variance[9]:.2%}")

# Visualize explained variance
fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 4))

# Plot 1: Individual variance per component
axes[0].bar(range(1, 51), pca_50.explained_variance_ratio_, 
            alpha=0.7, color='steelblue', edgecolor='black')
axes[0].set_xlabel('Principal Component', fontsize=12)
axes[0].set_ylabel('Variance Explained', fontsize=12)
axes[0].set_title('Variance Explained by Each PC', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')
axes[0].grid(alpha=0.3)

# Plot 2: Cumulative variance
axes[1].plot(range(1, 51), cumulative_variance, marker='o', 
             linewidth=2, markersize=4, color='coral')
axes[1].axhline(y=0.95, color='red', linestyle='--', label='95% variance')
axes[1].set_xlabel('Number of Components', fontsize=12)
axes[1].set_ylabel('Cumulative Variance Explained', fontsize=12)
axes[1].set_title('Cumulative Variance Explained', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')
axes[1].legend()
axes[1].grid(alpha=0.3)

plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

# Find number of components for 95% variance
n_components_95 = np.argmax(cumulative_variance >= 0.95) + 1
print(f"\n💡 To explain 95% variance, we need {n_components_95} components")


# ### 4.2 Key Insight: The Curse of Dimensionality
# 
# **Important Discovery**: We tested clustering performance with different numbers of PCA components (10 to 200).
# 
# **Result**: 
# - ✅ **ARI score (weak label quality) is IDENTICAL** across all dimensions (0.4041)
# - ⚠️ **Silhouette score gets WORSE** as dimensions increase
# 
# | Components | Variance | ARI Score | Silhouette |
# |------------|----------|-----------|------------|
# | 10         | 42.5%    | 0.4041    | 0.2032 ⭐   |
# | 50 (ours)  | 73.5%    | 0.4041    | 0.1189     |
# | 200        | 92.9%    | 0.4041    | 0.0940 ❌   |
# 
# **What this means:**
# - Retaining only 42.5% variance (10 components) gives BETTER clustering than 92.9% (200 components)
# - More variance ≠ Better performance
# - Extra dimensions contain noise that hurts K-Means distance calculations
# - Our choice of 50 components balances clustering quality with features for neural network training
# 
# **Takeaway**: This is a classic demonstration that **less is often more** in machine learning!
# 
# ---
# 
# ### 4.3 t-SNE for 2D Visualization
# 
# **What is t-SNE?**
# - Non-linear dimensionality reduction technique
# - Preserves local neighborhood structure
# - Excellent for visualizing high-dimensional clusters in 2D/3D
# 
# **Parameters:**
# - `perplexity`: Balance between local and global structure (typical: 5-50)
# - `max_iter`: Number of iterations (more = better but slower)
# 
# **Note**: t-SNE can take several minutes on large datasets.

# In[5]:


